  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 243 AM CST Sun Nov 22 2026\n\nCurrently, the trough axis extends across the High Plains to our \nwest with the associated surface cold front draped across eastern \nKansas early this morning. Light showers linger along and ahead of \nthe boundary and will slowly clear the forecast area to the east \nover the next few hours. Behind the front, stout northerly winds \nwith gusts between 25-30 mph will be seen. Clearing skies and \nfalling temperatures will lag slightly behind the front. Mostly \nclear skies will be seen across much of the state by midday with \nafternoon highs reaching only into the 40s and lower 50s. Under \nclear skies tonight, low temperatures will fall into the 20s to \naround 30 degrees for all locations. Similar conditions are expected \nfor Monday as a short wave exits the area and brief zonal flow sets \nup over the region.\n\nA short wave will sweep across the region on Tuesday. Given the dry \nair mass and dominant high pressure in the wake of the previous \nsystem, minimal impacts are expected. Cloud cover will increase on \nMonday night and very light precipitation will be possible across \nthe western half of the state on Tuesday morning, likely in the form \nof snow flurries. It is not expected that any wintry precip will \nimpact the forecast area with this quick hitting system. However, \nthis wave will bring a reinforcing shot of cold air sending \ntemperatures tumbling on Tuesday night. Overnight lows for Tuesday \nand Wednesday night will dip into the lower 20s for most across the \nstate with highs only reaching into the low to middle 40s. \n\nRidging will begin to build in over the Plains for the end of the \nweek, leading to a slight warmup for the end of the forecast period. \nAfternoon highs for Thursday will reach to around 50 degrees, middle \n50s are expected for Friday, and highs close to 60s will be seen for \nSaturday. Light winds and dry conditions are generally expected for \nthe end of the forecast period and upcoming holiday weekend.",
